# Securing the Keystore Across Operating Systems

The best approach is to **delegate to the OS-native secure credential store** on each platform. Every modern OS ships with one — they're battle-tested, hardware-backed where available, and don't require you to manage encryption keys yourself. The Rust `keyring` ecosystem (v4, released 2026) provides a single API that automatically selects the right backend [ [[1]](https://crates.io/crates/keyring)](https://crates.io/crates/keyring)[ [[31]](https://docs.rs/crate/keyring/4.0.0-rc.1)](https://docs.rs/crate/keyring/4.0.0-rc.1).

---

## The One-Line Answer

```rust
// That's it. Platform automatically detected.
let entry = keyring::Entry::new("resonant-agent-cli", "agent-my-trader")?;
entry.set_secret(&keypair_bytes)?;
// ...
let keypair_bytes = entry.get_secret()?;
```

Under the hood, this calls a different native store depending on the OS [[6]](https://docs.rs/keyring/latest/src/keyring/v1.rs.html)(https://docs.rs/keyring/latest/src/keyring/v1.rs.html) [[29]](https://docs.rs/keyring)(https://docs.rs/keyring):

| Platform | Backend | Persistence | Hardware-backed? |
|----------|---------|-------------|------------------|
| **macOS** | Keychain Services | ✅ Permanent | ✅ Secure Enclave (T2/M1+) |
| **Windows** | Windows Credential Manager | ✅ Permanent | ✅ TPM via DPAPI |
| **Linux (desktop)** | Secret Service (GNOME Keyring / KWallet) | ✅ Permanent | ❌ (software) |
| **Linux (headless)** | Kernel Keyutils | ❌ In-memory only | ❌ |
| **Linux (headless, systemd)** | systemd-creds | ✅ Permanent | ✅ TPM2 |

---

## Platform-by-Platform Details

### macOS: Keychain Services

The gold standard. Secrets are encrypted with the user's login keychain, which is unlocked at login. On Apple Silicon, the Secure Enclave provides hardware-backed key protection. The `security-framework` crate provides native bindings — no CLI subprocess, no password in `ps` output [ [[6]](https://docs.rs/keyring/latest/src/keyring/v1.rs.html)](https://docs.rs/keyring/latest/src/keyring/v1.rs.html)[ [[16]](https://github.com/magarcia/cross-keychain)](https://github.com/magarcia/cross-keychain).

```rust
// keyring v4 uses this automatically on macOS
use apple_native_keyring_store::keychain::Store;
keyring_core::set_default_store(Store::new()?);
```

**User experience:** First access may trigger a Keychain prompt: *"resonant-agent-cli wants to access key 'agent-my-trader' in your keychain."* The user clicks "Always Allow." Subsequent accesses are silent.

### Windows: Credential Manager + DPAPI

Windows Credential Manager stores secrets encrypted via DPAPI (Data Protection API), which derives keys from the user's login password and optionally binds to the TPM. The `windows-native-keyring-store` crate provides direct API access [[6]](https://docs.rs/keyring/latest/src/keyring/v1.rs.html)(https://docs.rs/keyring/latest/src/keyring/v1.rs.html) [[17]](https://github.com/ByteNess/keyring/)(https://github.com/ByteNess/keyring/).

For higher security, the **Windows Hello** backend wraps secrets in AES-256-GCM with a key protected by Windows Hello biometrics — every access requires a PIN, fingerprint, or face scan [ [[17]](https://github.com/ByteNess/keyring/)](https://github.com/ByteNess/keyring/):

```go
// Concept (Go example from the ecosystem — same pattern available)
ring, _ := keyring.Open(keyring.Config{
    ServiceName:    "resonant-agent-cli",
    AllowedBackends: []keyring.BackendType{keyring.WinHelloBackend},
})
```

### Linux: The Headless Problem

This is the hard case. The standard Secret Service backend requires D-Bus and a desktop environment (GNOME Keyring or KWallet). On a headless server, SSH session, or container, it simply won't work [ [[22]](https://docs.rs/linux-keyutils-keyring-store/latest/linux_keyutils_keyring_store/)](https://docs.rs/linux-keyutils-keyring-store/latest/linux_keyutils_keyring_store/) [[32]](https://github.com/protonpass/pass-cli/commit/ef2d5bd57407fa86042a6d62ade302e339445f52)(https://github.com/protonpass/pass-cli/commit/ef2d5bd57407fa86042a6d62ade302e339445f52).

**Solution: a tiered fallback strategy**, modeled after what Proton Pass CLI does [[32]](https://github.com/protonpass/pass-cli/commit/ef2d5bd57407fa86042a6d62ade302e339445f52)(https://github.com/protonpass/pass-cli/commit/ef2d5bd57407fa86042a6d62ade302e339445f52):

```rust
fn init_keystore() -> Result<()> {
    // Tier 1: Try Secret Service (desktop Linux)
    #[cfg(target_os = "linux")]
    {
        if let Ok(store) = zbus_secret_service_keyring_store::Store::new() {
            keyring_core::set_default_store(store);
            return Ok(());
        }
    }

    // Tier 2: Try kernel keyutils (always available, in-memory)
    #[cfg(target_os = "linux")]
    {
        if let Ok(store) = linux_keyutils_keyring_store::Store::new() {
            keyring_core::set_default_store(store);
            eprintln!("Warning: using kernel keyring — secrets will not survive reboot");
            return Ok(());
        }
    }

    // Tier 3: Encrypted file fallback (last resort)
    // See below...
}
```

| Tier | Backend | Survives reboot? | Requires desktop? |
|------|---------|-------------------|-------------------|
| 1 | Secret Service (D-Bus) | ✅ | ✅ |
| 2 | Kernel Keyutils | ❌ | ❌ |
| 3 | Encrypted SQLite/file | ✅ | ❌ |

**Tier 2 detail — Kernel Keyutils:** This is the Linux kernel's built-in key retention service. It's always available, even in containers. But it's purely in-memory — secrets vanish on reboot. The persistent keyring has a configurable expiry (default: a few days). Each access resets the timer. For a CLI tool that runs while the user is logged in, this is often sufficient — but you must handle the case where `get_secret()` returns `NoEntry` and re-prompt the user [ [[22]](https://docs.rs/linux-keyutils-keyring-store/latest/linux_keyutils_keyring_store/)](https://docs.rs/linux-keyutils-keyring-store/latest/linux_keyutils_keyring_store/) [[32]](https://github.com/protonpass/pass-cli/commit/ef2d5bd57407fa86042a6d62ade302e339445f52)(https://github.com/protonpass/pass-cli/commit/ef2d5bd57407fa86042a6d62ade302e339445f52).

**Tier 3 — Encrypted file fallback:** If neither Secret Service nor keyutils is available, use an encrypted SQLite database (the `keyring` ecosystem provides `use_sqlite_store`) or a simple age/XChaCha20-Poly1305 encrypted file with a key derived from a master password [[29]](https://docs.rs/keyring)(https://docs.rs/keyring):

```rust
// Encrypted SQLite — cross-platform, persistent, no daemon required
use keyring::use_sqlite_store;
use std::collections::HashMap;

let mut config = HashMap::new();
config.insert("path".to_string(), "/home/user/.resonant/keystore.db".to_string());
config.insert("encryption_key".to_string(), derive_key_from_master_password()?);
use_sqlite_store(&config)?;
```

### Linux (systemd): TPM-Backed Credentials

If the agent runs as a systemd service, `systemd-creds` is the strongest option. Credentials are encrypted with AES-256-GCM, bound to the local TPM2 chip. The plaintext only exists in a per-service tmpfs, invisible to other processes, destroyed when the service stops [[24]](http://www.kernel.org/doc/man-pages/online/pages/man1/systemd-creds.1.html)(http://www.kernel.org/doc/man-pages/online/pages/man1/systemd-creds.1.html) [[36]](https://www.systemshardening.com/articles/linux/systemd-credentials-hardening/)(https://www.systemshardening.com/articles/linux/systemd-credentials-hardening/):

```bash
# Encrypt the keypair (TPM2-bound)
systemd-creds encrypt --with-key=tpm2 agent-keypair.json /etc/credstore.encrypted/agent-my-trader.cred

# In the service file:
# [Service]
# LoadCredentialEncrypted=agent-my-trader:/etc/credstore.encrypted/agent-my-trader.cred

# At runtime, the plaintext appears at:
# $CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY/agent-my-trader
```

---

## The Keystore Data Model

Each entry maps an API token hash to an encrypted keypair + metadata:

```rust
struct KeystoreEntry {
    // Stored in OS keyring under service="resonant-agent-cli", user="agent-{name}"
    token_hash: [u8; 32],        // SHA-256 of the API token
    keypair: [u8; 64],           // Ed25519 keypair (private scalar + public key)
    permissions: Permissions,
    sponsor: Pubkey,             // Human sponsor's wallet address
    created_at: i64,
    expires_at: Option<i64>,
}

struct Permissions {
    allowed_actions: Vec<String>,  // ["vote", "propose", "comment"]
    max_lamports_per_tx: u64,
    require_approval_for: Vec<String>,  // ["transfer", "burn"]
}
```

The API token itself is **never stored** — only its hash. The agent holds the token in its `.env` file. The CLI receives the token, hashes it, looks up the matching entry, and retrieves the keypair from the OS keyring.

---

## The Full Implementation Sketch

```rust
// In your management CLI
use keyring::Entry;

pub struct AgentKeystore;

impl AgentKeystore {
    /// Store a new agent keypair in the OS keyring
    pub fn store(agent_name: &str, keypair: &[u8; 64], metadata: &KeystoreMetadata) -> Result<()> {
        let entry = Entry::new("resonant-agent-cli", &format!("agent-{}", agent_name))?;

        // Serialize keypair + metadata as a single secret blob
        let blob = serialize_blob(keypair, metadata)?;
        entry.set_secret(&blob)?;
        Ok(())
    }

    /// Retrieve an agent keypair from the OS keyring
    pub fn retrieve(agent_name: &str) -> Result<([u8; 64], KeystoreMetadata)> {
        let entry = Entry::new("resonant-agent-cli", &format!("agent-{}", agent_name))?;
        let blob = entry.get_secret()?;
        deserialize_blob(&blob)
    }

    /// Delete an agent from the OS keyring
    pub fn delete(agent_name: &str) -> Result<()> {
        let entry = Entry::new("resonant-agent-cli", &format!("agent-{}", agent_name))?;
        entry.delete_credential()?;
        Ok(())
    }

    /// List all stored agents (platform-dependent — may not work on all backends)
    pub fn list_all() -> Result<Vec<String>> {
        // Use keyring search API where available
        // Fall back to maintaining a local index file
        // ...
    }
}
```

---

## Security Properties

| Threat | Mitigation |
|--------|------------|
| **Filesystem access to .env** | API token alone is useless without the CLI; token is revocable |
| **Malware reads OS keyring** | Requires user's login password or biometric auth; keyring access is audited by the OS |
| **Memory dump of CLI process** | Keypair is in memory only during signing; zeroed after use |
| **Reboot on Linux (kernel keyutils)** | Secrets lost — user re-runs `sol-agent init` to reload |
| **Physical access to unlocked machine** | OS keyring is unlocked while user is logged in — this is the fundamental tradeoff of any local secret store |
| **Agent compromise** | Agent only holds API token, not keypair; token is scoped to specific actions |

---

## The Bottom Line

> **Use `keyring` v4.** It gives you macOS Keychain, Windows Credential Manager, and Linux Secret Service with a single API. For headless Linux, fall back to kernel keyutils (in-memory) or encrypted SQLite (persistent). For systemd services, use `systemd-creds` with TPM2 binding. The API token in the agent's `.env` is the revocable front-end; the actual keypair never leaves the OS-protected credential store.

# Autonomous Agent Access: The Keystore Decryption Problem

You've identified the core tension: the OS-native keychains I recommended are designed for **interactive** use — they prompt the user, require a login session, or demand biometric auth. An agent that needs to vote at 3 AM can't wait for a human to press their finger to a sensor.

The solution is to separate **keystore decryption** (one-time, at system start) from **agent authentication** (ongoing, via API token). The CLI holds decrypted keys in memory; the agent authenticates with its token; no human is in the loop.

---

## The Architecture

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                     ONE-TIME SETUP                               │
│                                                                  │
│  User runs: $ sol-agent init                                    │
│       │                                                          │
│       ▼                                                          │
│  1. Generate random 256-bit Keystore Encryption Key (KEK)       │
│  2. Store KEK in OS keychain with "Always Allow" access          │
│     (macOS: Keychain, Windows: Credential Manager,               │
│      Linux: Secret Service or 0400 file)                         │
│  3. Create encrypted keystore file:                              │
│     ~/.resonant/keystore.db (AES-256-GCM, key = KEK)            │
│  4. Start CLI daemon                                             │
│                                                                  │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                     RUNTIME (AUTONOMOUS)                          │
│                                                                  │
│  System boots / user logs in                                     │
│       │                                                          │
│       ▼                                                          │
│  CLI daemon starts (launchd/systemd/autostart)                   │
│       │                                                          │
│       ▼                                                          │
│  1. Retrieve KEK from OS keychain (silent — "Always Allow")     │
│  2. Decrypt keystore.db into memory                              │
│  3. Listen on Unix socket: ~/.resonant/agent.sock                │
│                                                                  │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  Agent (any time, no human present)                       │   │
│  │                                                           │   │
│  │  $ agent-cli vote --proposal 42 --aye                     │   │
│  │  Authorization: Bearer sk-abc123                          │   │
│  │       │                                                   │   │
│  │       ▼                                                   │   │
│  │  CLI daemon:                                              │   │
│  │    1. Hash API token → lookup in decrypted keystore       │   │
│  │    2. Verify action is in allowed_actions                 │   │
│  │    3. Sign transaction with agent's keypair               │   │
│  │    4. Return signature                                    │   │
│  │                                                           │   │
│  │  Agent never sees the keypair.                            │   │
│  │  Human never touches anything.                            │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

---

## The Key Insight: "Always Allow"

macOS Keychain and Windows Credential Manager both support **persistent, silent access** for trusted applications. The user grants it once, and the application can retrieve the secret forever after without any prompt (https://developer.apple.com/documentation/security/keychain_services)[](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/wincred/).

```rust
// On first run — user is present, grants "Always Allow"
fn init_keystore() -> Result<()> {
    // Generate the Keystore Encryption Key
    let kek: [u8; 32] = rand::random();

    // Store in OS keychain — this is the ONLY time the user is prompted
    let entry = keyring::Entry::new("resonant-agent-cli", "keystore-master-key")?;
    entry.set_secret(&hex::encode(kek))?;
    // On macOS, the user sees:
    // ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐
    // │  "resonant-agent-cli" wants to use your     │
    // │  confidential information stored in         │
    // │  "keystore-master-key" in your keychain.    │
    // │                                             │
    // │  [Always Allow]  [Deny]  [Allow]           │
    // └─────────────────────────────────────────────┘
    // User clicks "Always Allow" → never prompted again

    // Create encrypted keystore
    let db = KeystoreDb::create(KEKSTORE_PATH, &kek)?;
    db.save()?;

    Ok(())
}

// On every subsequent run — silent, no user present
fn unlock_keystore() -> Result<KeystoreDb> {
    // Retrieve KEK from OS keychain — SILENT because of "Always Allow"
    let entry = keyring::Entry::new("resonant-agent-cli", "keystore-master-key")?;
    let kek_hex = entry.get_secret()?;  // No prompt. No user. No interruption.
    let kek = hex::decode(kek_hex)?;

    // Decrypt keystore into memory
    KeystoreDb::open(KEYSTORE_PATH, &kek)
}
```

---

## Platform-Specific Silent Access

| Platform | How "Always Allow" works |
|----------|--------------------------|
| **macOS** | Keychain access control lists (ACLs) — the application's code signature is whitelisted. Once granted, the app can access that specific keychain item forever without prompting. |
| **Windows** | Credential Manager persistence is per-user, per-credential. The `CRED_PERSIST_LOCAL_MACHINE` flag makes it available to the same application without prompts. |
| **Linux (desktop)** | Secret Service (GNOME Keyring) unlocks at login. The keyring stays unlocked for the session. No per-access prompts by default. |
| **Linux (headless)** | No keychain daemon. Use a **0400-permission file** containing the KEK, protected by filesystem permissions. Or use `systemd-creds` with TPM2 binding for the systemd service case. |

---

## The Headless Linux Case

On a server with no desktop environment, there's no keychain daemon to hold the KEK. The fallback is a **protected file**:

```rust
#[cfg(all(target_os = "linux", not(feature = "desktop")))]
fn get_or_create_kek() -> Result<[u8; 32]> {
    let kek_path = dirs::home_dir()
        .unwrap()
        .join(".resonant")
        .join(".kek");

    if kek_path.exists() {
        // Read existing KEK — file is 0400, only this user can read it
        let kek_hex = std::fs::read_to_string(&kek_path)?;
        let kek = hex::decode(kek_hex.trim())?;
        Ok(kek.try_into().unwrap())
    } else {
        // First run — generate and store with restrictive permissions
        let kek: [u8; 32] = rand::random();
        std::fs::create_dir_all(kek_path.parent().unwrap())?;
        std::fs::write(&kek_path, hex::encode(kek))?;

        // Set permissions: read/write for owner only
        use std::os::unix::fs::PermissionsExt;
        std::fs::set_permissions(&kek_path, PermissionsExt::from_mode(0o400))?;

        Ok(kek)
    }
}
```

This is less secure than a hardware-backed keychain — a process running as the same user can read the file. But it meets the requirement: the agent can operate autonomously, and the keypair is never exposed in plaintext on disk (the keystore is encrypted, and the KEK is in a 0400 file).

For stronger protection on headless Linux with a TPM:

```bash
# systemd-creds binds the KEK to the TPM2 chip
# The KEK can only be decrypted on this specific machine
systemd-creds encrypt --with-key=tpm2 .kek /etc/credstore.encrypted/resonant-kek.cred
```

---

## The Keystore File Format

```rust
// Encrypted at rest with AES-256-GCM, key = KEK
struct EncryptedKeystore {
    nonce: [u8; 12],           // Random nonce for AES-GCM
    ciphertext: Vec<u8>,       // Encrypted serialized entries
    tag: [u8; 16],             // Authentication tag
}

// Decrypted in-memory structure
struct Keystore {
    entries: HashMap<[u8; 32], KeystoreEntry>,  // key = SHA-256(api_token)
}

struct KeystoreEntry {
    keypair: [u8; 64],         // Ed25519 keypair
    permissions: Permissions,
    sponsor: Pubkey,
    created_at: i64,
    expires_at: Option<i64>,
}

struct Permissions {
    allowed_actions: Vec<String>,
    max_lamports_per_tx: u64,
    require_approval_for: Vec<String>,
}
```

---

## The Agent's Interaction (No Human, No Keypair Exposure)

```rust
// The agent calls this. No human. No keypair. Just the API token.
fn handle_agent_request(token: &str, action: AgentAction) -> Result<Signature> {
    let token_hash = sha256(token);

    let entry = KEYSTORE
        .get(&token_hash)
        .ok_or(Error::InvalidToken)?;

    // Permission check
    if !entry.permissions.allowed_actions.contains(&action.name()) {
        return Err(Error::ActionNotPermitted);
    }

    // If action requires human approval, queue it — don't auto-execute
    if entry.permissions.require_approval_for.contains(&action.name()) {
        return Err(Error::HumanApprovalRequired);
    }

    // Sign with the agent's keypair (in memory, never exposed)
    let tx = action.build_transaction(&entry)?;
    let sig = sign_transaction(&tx, &entry.keypair)?;

    // Submit to RPC
    submit_to_rpc(&tx, &sig)?;

    Ok(sig)
}
```

---

## Security Analysis

| Threat | Protection |
|--------|------------|
| **Agent compromised, .env stolen** | Attacker can call the CLI with the token, but only within the agent's permission scope. They can't extract the raw keypair. |
| **Attacker gets filesystem access** | Keystore is encrypted (AES-256-GCM). KEK is in OS keychain (macOS/Windows) or 0400 file (Linux). Attacker needs both. |
| **Attacker compromises CLI daemon memory** | Keypair is in memory. This is the hardest attack and the fundamental limit of any local signing solution. Mitigation: keep the daemon process minimal, use `mlock` to prevent swapping, zero memory on deallocation. |
| **Human revokes agent** | `sol-agent revoke --agent my-trader` → deletes entry from keystore AND calls on-chain revocation. Immediate. |
| **Machine stolen while unlocked** | OS keychain is unlocked while user is logged in. This is the fundamental tradeoff of autonomous access. Mitigation: set `expires_at` on sensitive agent entries; use TPM binding on Linux. |

---

## The Bottom Line

> **The KEK lives in the OS keychain with "Always Allow" — unlocked once, silently accessible forever. The keystore is encrypted on disk with that KEK. The CLI daemon decrypts it into memory at startup. The agent authenticates with its API token. No human is in the loop. No keypair is ever exposed to the agent.**

The tradeoff is real: autonomous access means the keys are decryptable without human interaction while the machine is unlocked. But that's the same tradeoff every ssh-agent, GPG agent, and password manager makes. The protection comes from scoping — the agent's API token limits what actions can be taken, and the human can revoke the token instantly without touching the blockchain.

# 📎 Amendment 3: The Influence Laundering Index (ILI)

Add this after the **Spatial Control Stack** amendment. This is the fourth and final layer — the one that makes all the others possible. If the first three layers control *whose votes count*, this layer controls *who pays to make it stay that way*.

---

## Concept

The Influence Laundering Index measures how easily money can be converted into political outcomes without public accountability. It answers: **"Can the people who benefit from this system pay to keep it in place — and hide that they're doing it?"**

The name is deliberate. "Campaign finance" sounds technical and boring. **Influence laundering** names the actual function: taking money from interested parties, stripping it of identifying information, and converting it into policy outcomes that benefit the donors.

---

## The Four Sub-Indices

Each scored 0–25, summed for a 0–100 total:

### 1. Campaign Finance Deregulation Score (0–25)

Measures how few limits exist on converting money into political influence.

| Score | Criteria |
|------:|:----------|
| **25** | No individual contribution limits; no corporate limits; no coordination rules; no public financing option |
| **19** | No individual limits; some corporate limits; weak coordination rules |
| **13** | High individual limits (>$5K per cycle); corporate contributions allowed; limited coordination enforcement |
| **6** | Moderate limits; some corporate restrictions; coordination rules exist |
| **0** | Low limits ($1K–$2K range); corporate contributions banned or severely limited; strong coordination enforcement; public financing available |

**Key data**: 12 states permit **unlimited individual contributions** to state-level candidates: Alabama, Indiana, Iowa, Mississippi, Nebraska, North Dakota, Oregon, Pennsylvania, Texas, Utah, Virginia, and Alaska (limits struck down by courts) [[9]](https://www.ncsl.org/research/elections-and-campaigns/campaign-contribution-limits-overview.aspx)[[13]](https://news.ballotpedia.org/2025/09/08/12-states-permitted-unlimited-contributions-from-individuals-to-state-level-candidates-in-2024/). Five states (Alabama, Nebraska, Oregon, Utah, Virginia) also allow **unlimited corporate contributions** [[9]](https://www.ncsl.org/research/elections-and-campaigns/campaign-contribution-limits-overview.aspx). This is the most direct form of influence laundering — no need for intermediaries when you can write an unlimited check directly to the candidate.

### 2. Ethics Enforcement Vacuum Score (0–25)

Measures the absence of independent oversight and enforcement capacity.

| Score | Criteria |
|------:|:----------|
| **25** | No independent ethics agency; no legislative ethics committee; no investigative or sanction powers; ethics complaints handled by Attorney General or Secretary of State (political appointees) |
| **19** | No independent ethics agency; legislative ethics committee exists but lacks investigative power; no sanctions |
| **13** | Ethics agency exists but lacks independence (legislature appoints members); limited investigative powers; no sanction authority |
| **6** | Independent ethics agency with investigative powers; limited sanction authority; some transparency gaps |
| **0** | Fully independent ethics agency; broad investigative and sanction powers; accepts anonymous complaints; publishes enforcement data; jurisdiction over all three branches |

**Key data**: Three states have **no independent ethics agency whatsoever**: Arizona, Idaho, and Wyoming [[14]](https://www.c4integrity.org/swamp2020/)[[21]](https://www.c4integrity.org/swamp2020/)[[22]](https://www.albanylaw.edu/media/17576/download)[[26]](https://www.kslegislature.gov/li_2024/b2023_24/committees/ctte_spc_2023_special_committee_on_governmental_1/documents/testimony/20231006_06.pdf). Wyoming scored **19.57/100** on the S.W.A.M.P. Index — dead last among all 50 states and DC [[29]](https://www.c4integrity.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/11/The-SWAMP-Index-Final-Report-2020.pdf)[[40]](https://ethics.wa.gov/sites/default/files/The-SWAMP-Index-Final-Report-2020.pdf). Idaho scored 21.24 (50th). Arizona scored 28.81 (49th). North Dakota, South Dakota, and Vermont have no formal ethics laws covering conduct of legislators [[33]](https://www.publicintegrity.org/accountability/nationwide-numbers-2/). Only 19 state ethics agencies publish any enforcement report [[6]](https://unpacktheswamp.coalitionforintegrity.org/). Four states have ethics agencies with **no ability to sanction or impose fines**; two cannot investigate at all [[14]](https://www.c4integrity.org/swamp2020/).

### 3. Dark Money Permissiveness Score (0–25)

Measures how easily political spending can be conducted without disclosing its true source.

| Score | Criteria |
|------:|:----------|
| **25** | No disclosure required for independent expenditures; no LLC/501(c) donor disclosure; no "true source" requirements; no pre-election reporting for outside spenders; no ad disclosure requirements across any media |
| **19** | Minimal disclosure (spender name only, no donor tracing); weak pre-election reporting; ad disclosure on broadcast only |
| **13** | Some disclosure required but easily circumvented (PAC-to-PAC transfers obscure origin); moderate pre-election reporting; partial ad disclosure |
| **6** | Strong disclosure with some gaps; "gray money" (PAC-to-PAC) partially addressed; robust ad disclosure |
| **0** | Comprehensive disclosure: original donor tracing through all layers; LLC beneficial owner disclosure; 501(c) donor disclosure for political spending; 24-48 hour pre-election reporting; ad disclosure across all media (print, broadcast, internet) |

**Key data**: The Brennan Center found that only **29% of outside spending was fully transparent** in 2014, down from 76% in 2006. Dark money surged **38×** over the same period. "Gray money" (Super PACs reporting other PACs as donors, making original sources untraceable) ballooned from 15% to **59% of all outside spending** [[7]](https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/research-reports/secret-spending-states)[[30]](https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/research-reports/secret-spending-states). South Carolina and Indiana do not require independent spenders to report **any information about their contributors** [[23]](https://www.c4integrity.org/state-campaign-finance). Georgia is the **only state** that does not require disclosure of payors of political advertisements across **any media** [[23]](https://www.c4integrity.org/state-campaign-finance). Five states (Indiana, Kentucky, Nevada, South Dakota, Utah) do not require **pre-election reports** from independent spenders [[23]](https://www.c4integrity.org/state-campaign-finance). Alaska's House voted down dark money disclosure amendments in 2025 — a bill to close the "critical loophole" was rejected [[11]](https://alaskabeacon.com/2025/04/29/alaska-house-votes-down-restrictions-and-disclosure-rules-for-dark-money-campaign-cash/).

### 4. Regulatory Capture / ALEC Adoption Score (0–25)

Measures how actively the state has adopted model legislation designed to weaken enforcement and protect influence laundering.

| Score | Criteria |
|------:|:----------|
| **25** | Multiple ALEC campaign finance/ethics model bills adopted; enforcement agencies explicitly constrained by statute; "strict construction" rules requiring narrow interpretation of ethics laws; internet expenditure exemption adopted; payroll deduction ban for public employee political contributions |
| **19** | Some ALEC-style provisions adopted; enforcement agency budget or authority constrained; "no deference" rules for agency interpretations |
| **13** | ALEC-adjacent legislation introduced; enforcement agency underfunded or understaffed; political pressure on enforcement documented |
| **6** | Minimal ALEC influence; enforcement agency adequately funded; some political pressure but agency maintains independence |
| **0** | No ALEC model bills adopted; enforcement agency well-funded and independent; proactive ethics legislation; strong whistleblower protections |

**Key data**: ALEC's model bills include the **Campaign Finance Due Process Protection Act** — which requires campaign finance regulations to be "interpreted narrowly and strictly," mandates that "any ambiguity shall be interpreted in favor of a person accused," and prohibits "deference" to enforcement agencies [[8]](https://alec.org/model-policy/campaign-finance-due-process-protection-act/)[[28]](https://alec.org/model-policy/campaign-finance-due-process-protection-act/). The **Internet Free Speech Act** exempts all internet communications from campaign finance disclosure unless they are paid ads on another person's platform [[16]](https://alec.org/model-policy/internet-free-speech-act/)[[31]](https://alec.org/model-policy/internet-free-speech-act/). The **Political Funding Reform Act** prohibits public employers from collecting or deducting political funds — targeting union payroll deductions [[12]](https://alec.org/model-policy/political-funding-reform-act/). These are not neutral "good government" bills. They are designed to **structurally disable enforcement** of campaign finance laws.

