## 🎯 The Browser Ring Structure (Revised)

Since browser nodes can only measure RTT to peers they have DataChannels with, the ring structure is built differently:

```javascript
class BrowserMeridianNode {
  constructor(supernodeDc) {
    this.supernodeDc = supernodeDc;  // Connection to native supernode
    this.peers = new Map();           // peerId → { dc, rtt, ring }
    this.rings = Array(9).fill(null).map(() => []);
  }

  async addPeer(peerId, dataChannel) {
    // Measure RTT via DataChannel ping
    const rtt = await measureRtt(dataChannel);

    // Calculate ring
    const ringIndex = Math.floor(Math.log2(rtt));

    // Store
    this.peers.set(peerId, { dc: dataChannel, rtt, ring: ringIndex });
    this.rings[ringIndex].push(peerId);

    // If ring is over capacity (8 nodes), run hypervolume replacement
    if (this.rings[ringIndex].length > 8) {
      await this.optimizeRing(ringIndex);
    }
  }

  async findClosestNode(targetIp) {
    // Delegate to supernode for actual probing
    this.supernodeDc.send(JSON.stringify({
      type: 'query',
      target: targetIp
    }));

    // Supernode returns the closest peer
    const result = await this.waitForMessage('query_result');
    return result.closestPeerId;
  }
}
```

