## 🧪 The Revised Probe Protocol (Browser-Friendly)

```
Browser Node A wants to find closest node to Target T
  │
  │  Step 1: Query sent to supernode via DataChannel
  ├─→ Supernode S
  │
  │  Step 2: Supernode runs Meridian query routing (native)
  │     S probes its ring members via STUN (raw UDP)
  │     Finds candidate peers close to T
  │
  │  Step 3: Supernode asks candidates to confirm via browser-friendly method
  ├─→ Candidate Peer B (browser)
  │     B measures RTT to T via:
  │       • Existing DataChannel (if T is a known peer)
  │       • Ephemeral WebRTC connection (if T is a peer)
  │       • Reports "cannot measure" (if T is a web server)
  │
  │  Step 4: Supernode aggregates results
  │     Returns closest peer to A
  │
  └─→ Browser Node A establishes media connection to closest peer
```

For the case where the target is a **web server** (not a Meridian peer), the supernode handles it natively:

```jsonc
// Browser query for closest node to a web server
{
  "type": "query",
  "query_type": "closest_node",
  "target": "93.184.216.34:80",  // example.com
  "target_type": "http",          // supernode will use HTTP HEAD timing
  "requester": "browser-peer-A"
