## 🧪 The Real Challenge: Probe Latency for Ephemeral Connections

The ~500ms-2s cost of establishing an ephemeral WebRTC connection for probing is the real bottleneck. Here's how to mitigate it:

### Strategy 1: Warm Connection Pool

```javascript
class ConnectionPool {
  constructor(maxSize = 10) {
    this.pool = [];  // { target, pc, dc, lastUsed }
  }

  async getConnection(targetId) {
    // Reuse existing connection if available
    const existing = this.pool.find(c => c.target === targetId);
    if (existing) {
      existing.lastUsed = Date.now();
      return existing.dc;
    }

    // Create new connection
    const { pc, dc } = await establishEphemeralConnection(targetId);
    this.pool.push({ target: targetId, pc, dc, lastUsed: Date.now() });

    // Evict oldest if over capacity
    if (this.pool.length > maxSize) {
      const oldest = this.pool.reduce((a, b) => a.lastUsed < b.lastUsed ? a : b);
      oldest.pc.close();
      this.pool.splice(this.pool.indexOf(oldest), 1);
    }

    return dc;
  }
}
```

Keep the last 10 ephemeral connections warm. If a peer is probed again within a few minutes, the connection is reused.

### Strategy 2: Piggyback on Gossip

Meridian's gossip protocol already exchanges peer lists. **Use the gossip cycle to pre-establish connections** to newly discovered peers:

```javascript
// During gossip processing
async onGossipReceived(gossipMsg) {
  for (const peerId of gossipMsg.ring_samples) {
    if (!this.peers.has(peerId) && !this.pendingConnections.has(peerId)) {
      // Pre-establish connection in background (don't wait)
      this.pendingConnections.add(peerId);
      this.establishDataChannel(peerId).then(dc => {
        const rtt = await measureRtt(dc);
        this.addToRing(peerId, dc, rtt);
        this.pendingConnections.delete(peerId);
      });
    }
  }
}
```

By the time a query arrives, the connection is already established. **Probe cost: ~1ms (DataChannel ping).**

### Strategy 3: Parallel Probing with Timeout

Meridian already handles this — the paper says:

> *"Nodes that take more than ε·δ to provide an answer are ignored, as they cannot be closer to the target than the source."*

Set ε = 2. If an ephemeral probe takes > 2× the current node's RTT to the target, the result is discarded. This naturally handles slow probes.

