## Timing Wheel + EBF: The Simple Design

### Core Idea

Each EBF entry gets a **TTL**. A timing wheel efficiently manages expiration. When time advances past an entry's TTL, it's deleted from the EBF. If an entry is "refreshed" (the same block is requested again), its timer resets.

```
                    ┌─────────────────────────────┐
                    │       TIMING WHEEL           │
                    │  ┌───┬───┬───┬───┬───┐      │
                    │  │ 0 │ 1 │ 2 │...│ N │ ← hand│
                    │  └───┴───┴───┴───┴───┘      │
                    │    ↑                         │
                    │  current slot                │
                    │  contains list of EBF refs   │
                    │  to expire now               │
                    └──────────┬──────────────────┘
                               │ expires entries in
                               ▼
                    ┌─────────────────────────────┐
                    │   ELASTIC BLOOM FILTER       │
                    │  ┌───────────────────────┐   │
                    │  │ Bloom filter (fast)   │   │
                    │  ├───────────────────────┤   │
                    │  │ Bucket[0]: {fp₁, fp₂} │   │
                    │  │ Bucket[1]: {fp₃}      │   │
                    │  │ Bucket[2]: {}          │   │
                    │  │ ...                    │   │
                    │  └───────────────────────┘   │
                    └─────────────────────────────┘
```

---

### Data Structures

```typescript
interface TimedFingerprint {
  fp: number;              // the elastic fingerprint value
  wheelSlot: number;       // which timing wheel slot it expires in
  insertedAt: number;      // timestamp (for refresh decisions)
}

interface EBFBucket {
  fingerprints: TimedFingerprint[];
  // max size D as per EBF design
}

interface TimingWheel {
  slots: Array<Set<EBFEntryRef>>;  // each slot holds refs to expire
  slotCount: number;                // N
  slotDuration: number;             // how long each slot represents (e.g., 60s)
  currentSlot: number;              // hand position
  lastAdvance: number;              // last time the hand moved
}

// A reference into the EBF for the timing wheel to use
interface EBFEntryRef {
  bucketIndex: number;      // which bucket in the EBF
  fpIndex: number;          // which fingerprint within that bucket
}
```

