Exactly — that's the clean version. No separate "refresh" operation, no tracking `insertedAt` timestamps. Just:

**If the fingerprint is already in the bucket, move its expiry slot forward. If it's new, insert and schedule.**

```typescript
function insertMemory(ebf: EBF, wheel: TimingWheel, blockId: Hash): void {
  const { fp, index } = hashToEBF(blockId, ebf.size);
  const bucket = ebf.buckets[index];

  // Already remembered? Just push expiry forward.
  const existing = bucket.fingerprints.find(f => f.fp === fp);
  if (existing) {
    wheel.slots[existing.wheelSlot].delete(existing.ref);
    existing.wheelSlot = computeExpirySlot(wheel);
    wheel.slots[existing.wheelSlot].add(existing.ref);
    return;
  }

  // New memory.
  const slot = computeExpirySlot(wheel);
  const ref = { bucketIndex: index, fpIndex: bucket.fingerprints.length };
  bucket.fingerprints.push({ fp, wheelSlot: slot, ref });
  wheel.slots[slot].add(ref);
  ebf.bloomFilter.setBit(index);
}
```

The maintenance tick just advances the hand and clears whatever's in the current slot:

```typescript
function maintenanceTick(ebf: EBF, wheel: TimingWheel): void {
  wheel.currentSlot = (wheel.currentSlot + 1) % wheel.slotCount;
  for (const ref of wheel.slots[wheel.currentSlot]) {
    ebf.buckets[ref.bucketIndex].fingerprints.splice(ref.fpIndex, 1);
    // if bucket empties → clear Bloom bit
  }
  wheel.slots[wheel.currentSlot].clear();
}
```

That's the whole thing. The EBF doesn't need to know about time at all — it just stores fingerprints and exposes bucket access. The timing wheel owns the "when does this go away" concern entirely. A re-insert is just a reschedule.

