## How the Pieces Connect

### 1. Popularity-Weighted Random Selection

The `ranks` hashmap is the key. When a node needs to pick blocks for a new file representation:

```c
// Pick a random block biased toward higher popularity
index_entry_t* pick_block_for_representation(index_t* index) {
  // Walk ranks from highest fib downward
  // At each rank, use roulette-wheel or just pick uniformly from that bucket
  // Higher fib = exponentially more likely to be chosen

  uint32_t max_fib = find_max_fib(index->ranks);
  for (uint32_t fib = max_fib; fib > 0; fib--) {
    index_entry_vec_t* bucket = hashmap_get(&index->ranks, &fib);
    if (bucket != NULL && bucket->length > 0) {
      // Higher ranks have higher probability of being selected
      if (rand() % fibonacci(fib + 1) < fibonacci(fib)) {
        return bucket->data[rand() % bucket->length];
      }
    }
  }
  // Fallback: pick from fib=0
  return pick_from_fib_zero(index);
}
```

This ensures the most proven, widely-replicated blocks are used in new representations, maximizing the chance that retrieval nodes already have the constituent blocks.

