### 3. Respiration Driven by Fibonacci Ranks

**Inhale (0-50%):** Node seeks blocks. When evaluating offers, it compares the offered block's fib against its own `ranks`:

```c
bool should_pull(block_offer_t* offer, index_t* my_index) {
  // Always pull if we have room and block is new
  if (index_peek(my_index, offer->hash) == NULL) return true;

  // If we already have it, only pull if remote rank > local rank
  index_entry_t* local = index_find(my_index, offer->hash);
  return offer->fib > local->counter.fib;
}
```

**Exhale (80%+):** Node sheds blocks. It walks `ranks` from **lowest fib upward**:

```c
void exhale_blocks(index_t* index, block_cache_t* cache) {
  uint32_t fib = 0;
  size_t to_free = blocks_to_reach_target(index, 0.50);

  while (to_free > 0) {
    index_entry_vec_t* bucket = hashmap_get(&index->ranks, &fib);
    if (bucket != NULL) {
      for (size_t i = 0; i < bucket->length && to_free > 0; i++) {
        index_entry_t* entry = bucket->data[i];
        // Push to a node below 50%
        push_block_to_network(cache, entry);
        index_remove(index, entry->hash);
        to_free--;
      }
    }
    fib++;
  }
}
```

Low-fib blocks get pushed out first. High-fib blocks stay. The network naturally retains popular content and sheds obscure content toward nodes that have room.

