## Learning Rules Applied on Success

When a Find_Value succeeds (the holder is found), the response carries the full path back. Every node along the path applies Hebbian updates:

```plaintext
HANDLE-FIND-VALUE-RESPONSE(msg, socket):
  if not msg.found:
    // Path nodes get slight decay for the specific forwarding decisions?
    // SoPPSoN doesn't penalize failed searches — only absence of stimulus decays.
    // But we could apply a small negative feedback:
    APPLY-FAILURE-DECAY(msg.path)
    return

  // ── SUCCESS PATH ──
  // msg.path = [A, B, C, D] where A=requester, D=holder
  // msg.holder = D

  τ = msg.latency   // total search time
  T = MAX_SEARCH_TIME

  for i = 0; i < msg.path_len - 1; i++:
    forwarder = msg.path[i]
    forwardee = msg.path[i + 1]

    if forwarder == self.id:
      // ── FREQUENCY RULE (if I'm the requester) ──
      // Only applies to the original requester → holder chain
      // Actually, Frequency rule applies to requester→provider
      // Feedback rule applies to intermediate forwarders

      if i == 0:
        // I'm the original requester
        // Frequency rule: w_{self → holder} gets the full update
        Δw = γ · x_self · x_holder
        w_{self → holder} += Δw

        // Also update w_{self → first_hop} since they routed well
        w_{self → msg.path[1]} += η_f · Δw

      // FEEDBACK RULE: I forwarded to forwardee, and the search succeeded
      // w_{forwarder → forwardee} += η_f · Δw_original
      // But we need Δw_original from the requester
      // In practice, each node applies feedback based on the response quality

    // SYMMETRY RULE: if w_{forwarder → forwardee} was updated,
    // also update w_{forwardee → forwarder} by η_s · Δw
```

Actually, let me simplify. The SoPPSoN paper is clearer about this. Here's the correct application:

```plaintext
HANDLE-FIND-VALUE-RESPONSE(msg, socket):
  if not msg.found:
    // No weight penalty, but the absence of success means
    // no positive reinforcement either. Decay handles the rest.
    return

  // msg.path = [requester, hop1, hop2, ..., holder]
  // The last element is the holder (who has the block)

  requester = msg.path[0]
  holder = msg.path[msg.path_len - 1]

  τ = msg.latency
  q = 1.0   // quality: block was found, data validated on receipt

  // Base weight delta for the requester→holder relationship
  γ = γ₀ · (1 - τ / T) · q
  Δw = γ · 1 · 1   // x_requester = 1, x_holder = 1

  // ── FREQUENCY RULE ──
  // Applied to requester → holder
  // "The strength of the connection from peer i (requester) to j (holder)
  //  is updated when j provides required resources to i"
  if self.id == requester:
    w_{self → holder} += Δw
    // If no prior connection, create one with initial strength = Δw

  // ── FEEDBACK RULE ──
  // "When a resource search involves more than one peer, all the peers
  //  contributing to the search will update their connections"
  // w_kl += η_f · Δw_ij
  // where k is any peer involved except the original requester,
  // and l is the peer to which k forwarded the request
  for i = 1; i < msg.path_len - 1; i++:
    if self.id == msg.path[i]:
      forwarder = msg.path[i]
      forwardee = msg.path[i + 1]
      w_{forwarder → forwardee} += η_f · Δw

  // ── SYMMETRY RULE ──
  // "If peer i is related to peer j, then peer j is possibly related to peer i"
  // w_ji += η_s · Δw_ij
  // Applied to all updated connections
  for i = 0; i < msg.path_len - 1; i++:
    if self.id == msg.path[i]:
      forwarder = msg.path[i]
      forwardee = msg.path[i + 1]
      w_{forwardee → forwarder} += η_s · Δw
```

