## Store vs. Find_Value: The Duality

| | Find_Value | Store |
|---|---|---|
| **Goal** | Locate block X | Place block X |
| **Routes toward** | Node that holds X | Node that should hold X |
| **Selection weight** | Hebbian w (proven useful) | Composite suitability score |
| **Success** | Block found | Block accepted for storage |
| **Feedback** | Provider was useful | Storer was useful |
| **Failure** | Remember in EBF for recall | Try different targets |

---

## Composite Storage Suitability Score

A node's fitness to store a block combines four factors:

\[
S(j) = w_{i \rightarrow j} \cdot \underbrace{(1 - c_j)}_{\text{room}} \cdot \underbrace{\frac{1}{1 + \text{latency}(i,j)/\beta}}_{\text{proximity}} \cdot \underbrace{a_j}_{\text{availability}}
\]

Where:

| Symbol | Meaning | Range |
|--------|---------|-------|
| \(w_{i \rightarrow j}\) | Hebbian connection strength | \(\) |
| \(c_j\) | Current capacity fraction | \(\) |
| \(\text{latency}(i,j)\) | Measured RTT (ms) | \(\mathbb{R}^+\) |
| \(\beta\) | Latency normalization constant (e.g., 50 ms) | — |
| \(a_j\) | Uptime/availability score | \(\) |

This gives a score where:
- **High weight** → trusted peer, boosts score
- **Low capacity** → lots of room, boosts score
- **Low latency** → fast future retrievals, boosts score
- **High availability** → block stays online, boosts score

A node at 95% capacity with 300 ms latency and 60% uptime gets \(S \approx 0\). A node at 20% capacity with 5 ms latency and 99.9% uptime gets \(S\) near maximum.

---

## Availability Tracking

Uptime isn't something you can measure in one probe. It requires history. Each node tracks a simple EWMA (exponentially weighted moving average) for every peer:

```c
typedef struct {
  float     availability;       // EWMA [0, 1]
  uint64_t  last_seen;          // last successful ping timestamp
  uint32_t  consecutive_fails;  // how many pings missed
  uint64_t  first_seen;         // when we first discovered this peer
  uint64_t  total_observed_ms;  // total time we've been tracking
  uint64_t  total_online_ms;    // total time peer was reachable
} peer_availability_t;
```

Updated on every successful ping or gossip exchange:

```c
void update_availability(peer_availability_t* av, bool reachable) {
  uint64_t now = get_time_ms();

  if (reachable) {
    av->last_seen = now;
    av->consecutive_fails = 0;
  } else {
    av->consecutive_fails++;
  }

  // Recompute EWMA periodically
  uint64_t elapsed = now - av->last_availability_update;
  if (elapsed > AVAILABILITY_UPDATE_INTERVAL_MS) {
    float instant = reachable ? 1.0 : 0.0;
    float alpha = 0.1;  // smoothing factor
    av->availability = alpha * instant + (1 - alpha) * av->availability;
    av->last_availability_update = now;
  }
}
```

New peers start with \(a_j = 0.5\) (neutral, wait and see). Peers that have been observed for a long time with high uptime get \(a_j \rightarrow 1.0\). Peers that frequently disappear get \(a_j \rightarrow 0\).

