## 3. The Control Plane

### 3.1 The Attribute Machine

The Attribute Machine is the core of the control plane. It is a CRABS (Cryptographic Attribute-Based State Machine)  implementation whose state is the set of all users and their attributes.

Traditional ABE systems  require a central authority holding the Master Secret Key (MSK) to issue attributes. The Attribute Machine eliminates this by treating attributes as **protocol-governed state** rather than static cryptographic material. Changing an attribute is a state transition that follows the same rules as any other operation.

#### 3.1.1 Data Structure

```c
typedef struct {
    uint8_t     user_id[32];        /* SHA-256(public_key) */
    uint8_t     attributes[1024];   /* OR-Set CRDT (serialized) */
    uint64_t    attr_count;
    uint8_t     public_key[32];     /* Ed25519 */
    uint64_t    key_version;        /* ABE key version */
    uint8_t     status;             /* active, suspended, deceased */
} am_user_t;

typedef struct {
    char        operation[64];       /* e.g., "credit_account" */
    char        policy[256];         /* ABE policy expression */
} am_policy_t;

typedef struct {
    am_user_t   *users;
    uint64_t    user_count;
    am_policy_t *policies;
    uint64_t    policy_count;
    uint64_t    version;            /* CRDT version vector */
    uint8_t     node_id[32];
} attribute_machine_t;
```

#### 3.1.2 CRDT Merge

The Attribute Machine uses an OR-Set (Observed-Remove Set) CRDT  for attribute storage. Each attribute is tagged with a unique identifier (the granting operation's hash). When two replicas merge, the result is the union of all observed attributes minus all tombstoned attributes:

```c
/*
 * OR-Set CRDT merge for attributes
 *
 * Rule: An attribute exists in the merged set if it was
 * added in any replica and not removed in any replica
 * that has observed the add.
 *
 * This is convergent: merge(a, merge(b, c)) == merge(merge(a, b), c)
 */

int am_merge(attribute_machine_t *local,
             const attribute_machine_t *remote) {
    for (uint64_t i = 0; i < remote->user_count; i++) {
        am_user_t *remote_user = &remote->users[i];
        am_user_t *local_user = find_user(local, remote_user->user_id);

        if (!local_user) {
            /* New user — add entirely */
            add_user(local, remote_user);
            continue;
        }

        /* Merge attributes: union of observed, minus tombstoned */
        orset_merge(&local_user->attributes, &remote_user->attributes);

        /* Key version: max */
        if (remote_user->key_version > local_user->key_version)
            local_user->key_version = remote_user->key_version;
    }

    local->version = max(local->version, remote->version);
    return 0;
}
```

#### 3.1.3 Invoice-Scoped Attributes

The most common attribute grant in the Spark Ledger is the `can_credit:<invoice_id>` attribute, issued when a seller creates an invoice. This attribute is:

- **Scoped** — it applies only to the specific invoice
- **Targeted** — it is granted only to the designated buyer
- **One-time** — it is revoked when the invoice is paid or expired
- **Verifiable** — the buyer can prove they hold it without revealing other attributes

```c
int invoice_grant_attribute(attribute_machine_t *am,
                             const invoice_t *inv) {
    char attr[96];
    snprintf(attr, sizeof(attr), "can_credit:%s",
            hex_encode(inv->invoice_id, 32));
    return am_grant(am, inv->buyer_id, attr,
                   inv->seller_id, inv->seller_sig);
}

int invoice_revoke_attribute(attribute_machine_t *am,
                             const invoice_t *inv) {
    char attr[96];
    snprintf(attr, sizeof(attr), "can_credit:%s",
            hex_encode(inv->invoice_id, 32));
    return am_revoke(am, inv->buyer_id, attr,
                    inv->seller_id, inv->seller_sig);
}
```

### 3.2 Protocol State Machines

Every resource in the Spark Ledger follows a protocol state machine. The protocol defines valid state transitions; the ledger enforces them.

#### 3.2.1 Invoice Protocol

```plaintext
  DRAFT ──issue──► INVOICED ──credit──► PAID ──receipt──► RECEIPTED ──close──► IDLE
    │                  │                   │
    └──cancel──► IDLE   └──expire──► IDLE   └──dispute──► INVOICED
```

The invoice protocol ensures that:
- A buyer cannot credit a seller's ledger without a valid invoice
- An invoice cannot be paid twice (ONE_SHOT_FLAG)
- A receipt cannot be generated before payment
- The `can_credit` attribute is revoked after successful payment

#### 3.2.2 Contract Protocol

```plaintext
  DRAFT ──negotiate──► PROPOSED ──accept──► ACTIVE ──complete──► COMPLETED
    │                     │                   │
    └──cancel──► IDLE     └──reject──► IDLE   └──default──► DEFAULTED
```

The contract protocol ensures that:
- A contract cannot be paid before it is accepted
- Contract payments reduce the buyer's future daily credit
- Default is detected when a payment is missed
- The contract's state is visible to both parties

#### 3.2.3 Daily Reset Protocol

```plaintext
  IDLE ──midnight──► RESETTING ──apply──► IDLE
```

The daily reset is the mechanism that enforces credit expiration:

```c
/*
 * Algorithm: DAILY_RESET
 *
 * 1. Calculate unused daily issuance
 * 2. Discard unused issuance (it expires)
 * 3. Move spent issuance to cumulative_debt
 * 4. Reset daily_issuance counter to 0
 * 5. Calculate contract deductions for tomorrow
 * 6. Set available_credit = 86400 - contract_deductions
 * 7. Log the reset as a signed operation
 * 8. Update SMT root
 */

int execute_daily_reset(ledger_state_t *state,
                       daily_reset_t *reset_out) {
    account_entry_t daily, debt, income;
    uint8_t key_daily[32], key_debt[32], key_income[32];

    derive_account_key(key_daily, state->party.party_id, "daily_issuance");
    derive_account_key(key_debt, state->party.party_id, "cumulative_debt");
    derive_account_key(key_income, state->party.party_id, "cumulative_income");

    smt_get(&state->smt, key_daily, &daily);
    smt_get(&state->smt, key_debt, &debt);
    smt_get(&state->smt, key_income, &income);

    int64_t issuance_used = daily.balance;
    int64_t issuance_discarded = SPARKS_PER_DAY - issuance_used;

    /* Move spent issuance to cumulative debt */
    debt.balance += issuance_used;

    /* Reset daily counter */
    daily.balance = 0;

    /* Calculate contract deductions */
    int64_t deductions = calculate_contract_deductions(state);
    int64_t available = SPARKS_PER_DAY - deductions;

    /* Update SMT */
    smt_set(&state->smt, key_daily, &daily);
    smt_set(&state->smt, key_debt, &debt);

    /* Log */
    reset_out->issuance_used = issuance_used;
    reset_out->issuance_discarded = issuance_discarded;
    reset_out->new_debt = issuance_used;
    reset_out->contract_deductions = deductions;
    reset_out->available_credit = available;

    return 0;
}
```

### 3.3 ABE Encryption

Transaction payloads are encrypted under ABE policies, ensuring only authorized parties can decrypt. The ABE system uses Ciphertext-Policy ABE (CP-ABE) , where ciphertexts embed policies and user keys carry attributes.

When Alice sends a payment to Bob referencing invoice `inv_001`, the payment payload is encrypted under the policy `"has_attribute:can_credit:inv_001"`. Only a party holding that attribute (Alice) can decrypt the payload and construct the valid credit operation on Bob's ledger.

Key versioning ensures that when attributes change, old ABE keys are invalidated:

```c
int verify_key_is_current(const ledger_state_t *state,
                         const uint8_t user_id[32],
                         uint64_t claimed_version) {
    am_user_t *user = find_user(&state->attr_machine, user_id);
    if (!user) return -1;
    return (claimed_version == user->key_version) ? 0 : -1;
}
```

### 3.4 Guild Policy Engine

The Guild Policy Engine evaluates Smart Economic Policies before transaction construction. Each policy is a deterministic, sandboxed predicate that can approve, deny, or approve with conditions:

```c
typedef enum {
    POLICY_APPROVE     = 0,
    POLICY_DENY        = 1,
    POLICY_CONDITIONAL = 2
} policy_result_t;

typedef struct {
    uint8_t     type;       /* tax, donation, insurance, escrow, penalty */
    int64_t     amount;
    uint8_t     recipient[32];
    char        description[128];
} policy_condition_t;

typedef struct {
    policy_result_t result;
    char            reason[256];
    uint8_t         condition_count;
    policy_condition_t conditions[8];
} policy_eval_t;
```

The evaluation function checks all guilds the transacting parties belong to:

```c
/*
 * Algorithm: EVALUATE_GUILD_POLICIES
 *
 * 1. Get all guilds the seller belongs to
 * 2. Get all guilds the buyer belongs to
 * 3. For each guild, load subscribed policy bundles
 * 4. Evaluate each policy against the transaction
 * 5. If any policy returns DENY → block transaction
 * 6. Collect all conditions from CONDITIONAL results
 * 7. Return aggregated result
 */

int evaluate_guild_policies(const attribute_machine_t *am,
                            const transaction_t *tx,
                            policy_eval_t *result) {
    result->result = POLICY_APPROVE;
    result->condition_count = 0;

    /* Get seller's guilds */
    uint8_t seller_guilds[MAX_GUILDS][32];
    uint8_t seller_guild_count = get_guild_memberships(am, tx->payer_id,
                                                       seller_guilds);

    /* Get buyer's guilds */
    uint8_t buyer_guilds[MAX_GUILDS][32];
    uint8_t buyer_guild_count = get_guild_memberships(am, tx->payee_id,
                                                      buyer_guilds);

    /* Evaluate seller-side policies */
    for (int i = 0; i < seller_guild_count; i++) {
        guild_policy_set_t *policies = load_policies(seller_guilds[i]);
        for (int j = 0; j < policies->count; j++) {
            policy_eval_t eval;
            evaluate_single_policy(&policies->entries[j], tx, &eval);

            if (eval.result == POLICY_DENY) {
                *result = eval;
                return -1;  /* Blocked */
            }

            if (eval.result == POLICY_CONDITIONAL) {
                /* Aggregate conditions */
                for (int k = 0; k < eval.condition_count; k++) {
                    result->conditions[result->condition_count++] =
                        eval.conditions[k];
                }
            }
        }
    }

    /* Evaluate buyer-side policies (same pattern) */
    /* ... */

    if (result->condition_count > 0)
        result->result = POLICY_CONDITIONAL;

    return 0;
}
```

When conditions are present, the protocol automatically splits the payment:

```c
/*
 * Algorithm: APPLY_POLICY_CONDITIONS
 *
 * For a transaction of amount A with N conditions:
 *   condition_1.amount + condition_2.amount + ... + condition_N.amount = C
 *   seller_receives = A - C
 *
 * Each condition transfer is a separate signed operation
 * on the seller's ledger, executed atomically with the main payment.
 */

int apply_policy_conditions(ledger_state_t *state,
                            const transaction_t *tx,
                            const policy_eval_t *eval) {
    int64_t remaining = tx->amount;

    for (int i = 0; i < eval->condition_count; i++) {
        const policy_condition_t *cond = &eval->conditions[i];

        /* Create and execute condition transfer */
        transaction_t cond_tx = *tx;
        cond_tx.amount = cond->amount;
        memcpy(cond_tx.payee_id, cond->recipient, 32);
        cond_tx.type = (cond->type == 0) ? TX_GUILD_DUES :
                      (cond->type == 1) ? TX_DONATIONS :
                      TX_TRANSFER;

        execute_transaction(state, &cond_tx);
        remaining -= cond->amount;
    }

    /* Main payment with remaining amount */
    transaction_t main_tx = *tx;
    main_tx.amount = remaining;
    return execute_transaction(state, &main_tx);
}
```

