## 7. MGRS Progressive Location Disclosure

### 7.1 Design

MGRS (Military Grid Reference System) provides a hierarchical coordinate system where each additional digit narrows the location by a factor of 10. The Spark Ledger uses this hierarchy as a privacy mechanism:

```c
#define MGRS_MIN_INVOICE_PRECISION   4   /* 10km minimum on invoices */
#define MGRS_MIN_RECEIPT_PRECISION   2   /* 100km minimum on receipts */
#define MGRS_MAX_DISCLOSED          8   /* 100m maximum, ever */

int mgrs_truncate(const uint8_t full_mgrs[12], uint8_t precision,
                  uint8_t truncated[12]) {
    memcpy(truncated, full_mgrs, 12);
    for (int i = precision; i < 12; i++)
        truncated[i] = 0;
    return 0;
}

int mgrs_truncate_equals(const uint8_t full_mgrs[12],
                          const uint8_t claimed_mgrs[12],
                          uint8_t precision) {
    for (int i = 0; i < (int)precision; i++) {
        if (full_mgrs[i] != claimed_mgrs[i])
            return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}
```

### 7.2 Receipt Precision

The receipt reveals MGRS at the **minimum** of both parties' receipt precision settings:

```c
uint8_t receipt_precision(uint8_t seller_precision,
                         uint8_t buyer_precision) {
    uint8_t precision = min(seller_precision, buyer_precision);

    /* Enforce system minimums */
    if (precision < MGRS_MIN_RECEIPT_PRECISION)
        precision = MGRS_MIN_RECEIPT_PRECISION;
    if (precision > MGRS_MAX_DISCLOSED)
        precision = MGRS_MAX_DISCLOSED;

    return precision;
}
```

The STARK circuit verifies that the disclosed MGRS is an honest truncation of the full MGRS (Constraints 8 and 9). This prevents parties from lying about their location while still allowing them to control their disclosure precision.

