## 10. Security Analysis

### 10.1 Threat Model

| Adversary | Capabilities | Defense |
|-----------|-------------|---------|
| Network | Observe, delay, reorder, drop messages | ABE encryption, signatures |
| Byzantine node | Arbitrary behavior within a party's cluster | Raft consensus (enterprise) |
| Compromised user | Use stale keys, forge signatures, collude | Key versioning, nonce ordering |
| Malicious prover | Generate false STARK proofs | STARK verification (soundness) |
| Receipt spammer | Submit many valid receipts to Commonomicon | Receipt nonce deduplication |

### 10.2 Security Properties

| Property | Mechanism | Guarantee |
|----------|-----------|----------|
| Authorization | ABE policy verification | Only users with matching attributes can perform operations |
| Authentication | Pluggable signatures | Operations are bound to a specific key |
| Non-repudiation | Signed transition log | Past operations cannot be denied |
| Collusion resistance | ABE key randomization | Two users cannot combine keys to gain unauthorized access |
| Replay protection | Per-account nonces | Old messages cannot be replayed |
| Liveness | Lock timeout + force-unlock | No resource can be locked indefinitely |
| Confidentiality | ABE encryption | Only authorized parties can decrypt payloads |
| Privacy (Mode B) | Optional signer omission | Verifier learns only that *some* authorized user signed |
| Receipt integrity | STARK proof verification | Commonomicon can verify receipts without trusting the prover |
| Location honesty | STARK circuit constraints | Parties cannot lie about MGRS truncation |
| Anti-dynasty | Legacy distribution caps | Wealth cannot concentrate across generations |

### 10.3 STARK Soundness

The STARK proof system provides computational soundness with negligible error probability. For the chosen parameters (trace length 4096, 50 queries, blowup factor 8), the soundness error is approximately 2^{-150} — far below any practical threshold.

The transparency of STARKs means there is no trusted setup that could be compromised. The verification key is derived from the public circuit description. Anyone can audit the circuit, verify the key, and confirm that the proof system is sound.

