## Why CMake + CTest is Better Here 🏗️

| Feature | Plain Makefile | CMake + CTest |
|:---|---:|:---:|
| **Cross-platform** | ❌ Linux-only | ✅ Windows/macOS/Linux |
| **Dependency discovery** | ❌ Manual flags | ✅ `pkg_check_modules` auto-discovers |
| **Individual test selection** | ❌ All-or-nothing | ✅ `ctest -R <test_name>` |
| **Parallel test execution** | ❌ Manual | ✅ `ctest -j4` |
| **CI integration** | ❌ Custom scripts | ✅ Native GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, etc. |
| **Test output on failure** | ❌ Manual | ✅ `--output-on-failure` |
| **Install rules** | ❌ Manual | ✅ `install()` target |
| **IDE integration** | ❌ None | ✅ CLion, VS Code, Visual Studio |

The `--test=<name>` pattern lets CTest run each test as an **independent process**, which is important for:

1. **Isolation** — A crash in one test doesn't bring down others
2. **Memory leak detection** — Valgrind can be applied per-test
3. **Parallelism** — Independent tests can run simultaneously
4. **Clear failure reporting** — CTest tells you exactly which test(s) failed

