## The Complete Lock Protocol Grammar

```plaintext
                    ┌─────────────────────────────────────┐
                    │         LOCK PROTOCOL GRAMMAR         │
                    │                                      │
                    │  Valid sequences:                    │
                    │                                      │
                    │  Normal:                             │
                    │    __lock__ → op → __verify__ → __unlock__
                    │                                      │
                    │  Extension:                          │
                    │    __lock__ → __extend__ → op → ...  │
                    │                                      │
                    │  Rollback:                           │
                    │    __lock__ → op → __rollback__      │
                    │                                      │
                    │  Force unlock (anyone, after expiry): │
                    │    ... → __force_unlock__             │
                    │                                      │
                    │  Invalid:                            │
                    │    op → __lock__     (lock first!)   │
                    │    __unlock__ → op   (already unlocked)
                    │    __extend__ by non-owner (wrong!)  │
                    │                                      │
                    └─────────────────────────────────────┘
```

---

## The Built-In Primitives Summary

| Primitive | Who Can Call | When | Effect |
|:---|---:|:---:|:---|
| `__lock__` | Anyone with policy | Resource in `idle` | Acquires lock, saves snapshot |
| `__extend__` | Lock holder only | Lock active | Resets expiry timer |
| `__verify__` | Lock holder only | After operation | Checks invariants |
| `__rollback__` | Lock holder only | After operation, before verify | Restores pre-lock snapshot |
| `__unlock__` | Lock holder only | After verify | Releases lock normally |
| `__force_unlock__` | **Anyone** | Lock expired | Rolls back, releases lock |

---

## Why This Design Is Robust 🛡️

| Scenario | What Happens |
|:---|---:|
| 🟢 **Normal operation** | Lock → op → verify → unlock |
| 🔴 **Client crashes mid-operation** | Lock expires → anyone force-unlocks → state rolled back |
| 🔴 **Client goes offline after modifying** | Lock expires → force-unlock rolls back → no partial state |
| 🔴 **Malicious client holds lock forever** | Lock expires → force-unlock by anyone → resource freed |
| 🟡 **Operation needs more time** | Lock holder extends (up to limit) |
| 🟢 **Client recovers after force-unlock** | Their operation was rolled back — they retry |

The key insight: **force-unlock is a safety valve, not a normal path.** It should be rare. But its existence guarantees the system can never deadlock.

