## What Levien's Code Actually Does

The `ot_toy.js` is deceptively short (~200 lines) but packs in several profound ideas. Let me break it down:

### 1. The Core Data Structure: A Size-Balanced BST for Deleted Positions

```javascript
// A BST where each node tracks:
//   value: the position (in internal coordinates)
//   size:  total nodes in subtree
//   height: for balancing

function xi(tree, i)      // internal → visible index
function xi_inv(tree, i)  // visible → internal index
function union_one(tree, i) // add a deleted position
function xi_one(tree, i)    // shift positions >= i by +1 (for insertions)
```

This is the **key innovation**: a single balanced BST that maps between two coordinate spaces:

```plaintext
Internal indices (including deleted text):
  [0] [1] [2] [3] [4] [5] [6] [7] [8]
   H   e   l   l   o   W   o   r   l   d
                    ↑ deleted

Visible indices (user sees):
  [0] [1] [2] [3] [4] [5] [6] [7]
   H   e   l   l   o   r   l   d
                    ↑ 'W' is hidden

xi(tree, 5) = 5      // internal 5 → visible 5 (before deletion)
xi(tree, 6) = 5      // internal 6 → visible 5 (after deletion, shifted)
xi_inv(tree, 5) = 6  // visible 5 → internal 6
```

### 2. The Transform Function

```javascript
function transform(op1, op2) {
    if (op2.ty != 'ins') { return op1; }
    return transform_ins(op1, op2.ix, op2.pri);
}

function transform_ins(op1, ix, pri) {
    if (op1.ty == 'ins') {
        // Insert vs Insert: lower priority shifts for higher
        if (op1.ix < ix || (op1.ix == ix && op1.pri < pri)) {
            return op1;  // No adjustment needed
        }
        // op1 is at or after the insert, shift right
        return {ty: op1.ty, ix: op1.ix + 1, ...};
    } else { // op1.ty is 'del'
        if (op1.ix < ix) {
            return op1;  // Delete is before insert, no adjustment
        }
        // Delete is at or after the insert, shift right
        return {ty: op1.ty, ix: op1.ix + 1, ...};
    }
}
```

The **priority** system is the elegance: concurrent inserts at the same position are ordered deterministically by priority, so all peers converge to the same order.

### 3. The Peer Merge Algorithm

```javascript
class Peer {
    constructor() {
        this.rev = 0;           // How many ops we've confirmed
        this.context = new Set(); // Ops we've seen but can't confirm yet
    }

    merge_op(docState, op) {
        // Case 1: This is the next expected operation
        if (this.rev < ops.length && ops[this.rev].id == op.id) {
            this.rev++;
            // Check if any context ops can now be confirmed
            while (this.rev < ops.length && this.context.has(ops[this.rev].id)) {
                this.context.delete(ops[this.rev].id);
                this.rev++;
            }
            return;
        }

        // Case 2: We've already seen this op but can't advance
        for (var ix = this.rev; ix < ops.length; ix++) {
            if (ops[ix].id == id) {
                this.context.add(id);
                return;
            }
        }

        // Case 3: New operation — transform against all unseen ops
        // ... (the transform loop)
        docState.add(op);
    }
}
```

This is the **OT/CRDT synthesis**: operations are transformed against concurrent operations (OT), but the system converges without a central sequencer (CRDT).

