## How This Extends CRABS

### Current CRABS Limitation

CRABS data items are currently **typed but static** in their internal structure:

```plaintext
COUNTER:     value = 42
SET:         elements = {a, b, c}
REGISTER:    value = "hello"
DOCUMENT:    value = "hello" (RGA)
```

Even RGA (the most expressive built-in type) only supports **insert and delete at positions**. It cannot:
- Move a paragraph
- Swap two elements
- Transform one operation against another
- Preserve intent under complex concurrent edits

### Levien-Extended CRABS

The BST + transform approach generalizes to **any ordered data structure**:

```c
// A CRABS data item using Levien's approach
typedef struct {
    // The BST of "deleted" or "transformed" positions
    // (generalized: tracks all positional adjustments)
    crabs_bst_t *position_map;

    // The visible value (what users/operations see)
    crabs_value_t visible_value;

    // The full value (including hidden/deleted elements)
    crabs_value_t full_value;

    // Operation log (for transform context)
    crabs_ot_op_t *op_log;
    size_t op_count;

    // Priority counter (monotonic, per-node)
    uint64_t priority_counter;

} crabs_levien_item_t;
```

### Generalized Operations

```c
// Any operation on a Levien-extended data item
typedef struct {
    crabs_ot_op_type_t type;  // INSERT, DELETE, UPDATE, MOVE, SWAP

    // Position in VISIBLE coordinates
    uint64_t visible_pos;

    // Priority for deterministic ordering of concurrent ops
    uint64_t priority;        // (node_id << 48) | sequence_num

    // Payload
    crabs_value_t payload;

    // Unique ID for causal tracking
    crabs_op_id_t id;

} crabs_levien_op_t;
```

### The Generalized Transform

```c
// Transform op1 against op2 (op2 happened concurrently)
// Returns the adjusted version of op1
crabs_levien_op_t crabs_levien_transform(
    crabs_levien_op_t op1,
    crabs_levien_op_t op2
) {
    // The transform depends on the operation types
    switch (op2.type) {
        case INSERT:
            // An insert shifts positions after it
            if (op1.visible_pos < op2.visible_pos ||
                (op1.visible_pos == op2.visible_pos && 
                 op1.priority < op2.priority)) {
                return op1;  // No adjustment
            }
            // Shift right
            op1.visible_pos++;
            return op1;

        case DELETE:
            // A delete shifts positions after it left
            if (op1.visible_pos < op2.visible_pos) {
                return op1;  // Before the delete, no adjustment
            }
            // Shift left
            op1.visible_pos--;
            return op1;

        case MOVE:
            // A move is like a delete + insert
            // ... (more complex transform)

        case SWAP:
            // ... (even more complex)
    }
}
```

