## Best Practice Pattern

**Lazy Loading with Queue:**
```java
public class PubSubManager {
    private final BlockingQueue<Message> pendingMessages = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>();

    public void publish(Message msg) {
        if (service == null || !service.asBinder().isBinderAlive()) {
            pendingMessages.offer(msg);
            ensureRunningAndConnect(); // Try to start
        } else {
            try {
                service.publish(msg.topic, msg.data);
            } catch (RemoteException e) {
                pendingMessages.offer(msg);
            }
        }
    }

    private void onServiceConnected(IPubSubService svc) {
        // Flush pending messages
        while (!pendingMessages.isEmpty()) {
            Message msg = pendingMessages.poll();
            try {
                svc.publish(msg.topic, msg.data);
            } catch (RemoteException e) {
                pendingMessages.offer(msg); // Put back, retry later
                break;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

**Bottom line:** You can get 90% reliability with foreground service + whitelisting, but you must architect for the 10% case where the service is dead. Android simply doesn't allow guaranteed background execution for user apps.
